It's been a year since I [last did this](software-hills-i-will-die-on-2025), so I figured
it would be fun to turn this into a yearly little tradition. Especially since there's
always new stuff to ~rant~ write about! First, though, let's revisit (briefly) what I said
last year:

# 2025's Opinions
## "Using an LLM Coding Assistant Makes You A Worse Engineer"
To probably no one's surprise, [I'm still very much on this train]($link.sibling('software-hills-i-will-die-on-2025').ref('opinion1')). Many things have
happened in the world of LLM coding in the last year (mostly all the agentic loop stuff
that really kicked off 6-8 months ago), and yet I still consistently find myself
outputting software that is as good or better than many of my AI-pilled peers having never
touched a single Claude.md (or whatever the current "magic markdown cauldron chant" file
is). Going full agentic makes you a [bad programmer](https://aimakesmesad.com/bad-programmer/). Flag planted on hill.

This sounds very conceited and that's because it slightly is. I will temper with the fact
that I do think there is *some* value to your Claudes and Codexes and what-have-yous, but the
handful of times I've seen that value rear its head it has without exception been in a very
narrow, very well defined, very single-purpose context (pun intended). I tried the agentic
loop style for about a week when it became the new hot shit. I threw away everything it
did, because I knew I could do it better. Sure, they can "type" at the speed of light, but
I build systems that aren't a house of cards. Typing speed does not matter at all.

/end rant

## "Most Comments in Your Code Should Have a Callout Type"
I still think [this one]($link.sibling('software-hills-i-will-die-on-2025').ref('opinion2')) is true as well. Not much to
say about it really, other than that I continue to get a ton of utility out of it if for
no other reasons than searchability and visual delineation.

## "Pure TDD Is Mostly An Exercise In Frustration, Not Productivity"
I also still think [this]($link.sibling('software-hills-i-will-die-on-2025').ref('opinion3')) is true, though maybe with less strong feelings.
I happened to be writing the original post at a time when TDD was being shoved very hard
down my throat, and I naturally lashed back out against it. To be clear, I still think
that adhering to Test-Driven Dogma is generally just not a fun time, but I've also softened my
general opinions on how other people write code substantially (excepting the LLM thing).

## "Many SWEs Need to be Hit With an RTFM"
Hoo, boy. On the one hand, [this]($link.sibling('software-hills-i-will-die-on-2025').ref('opinion4')) was also driven by a current frustration at the time with
people using me as the "answer machine" instead of learning to use their tools correctly.
My better nature gremlin tells me I should tame this one and say something to the effect of "it's
ok, we're all always learning, you may be one of [today's lucky ten thousand](https://xkcd.com/1053/)."

Then the little gremlin on the other shoulder chimes in with the fact that now it seems
like every other person has determined that because they have a magic slot machine that
can shit out bash scripts for them, they don't need to even ask me in the first place and can
instead drop thousands of lines of slop that breaks the entire codebase on my lap every
morning. So, potato potato.

The enlightened gremlin that's meditated on inner peace and shit has come to the
conclusion that it's best to just link people documentation and let them read it and ask
questions (unless it is actually a question that merits initial discussion). Enlightened
gremlin will also just reject slop patches to his code, or quietly rewrite it to work
correctly a few weeks after it was merged without his approval. He's a bit of a
perfectionist.

# New Hills This Year

I thought long and hard about what I wanted to put on this list that I haven't said
already. I only came up with two, and neither is a particularly novel idea, but they are
things that I've found myself pretty firmly fighting for.

## [Build your systems as if they are going to run on 10 year old hardware, even if they aren't]($section.id('opinion1'))
My current role has me working with distributed clusters of machines that are all fairly
capable little boxes in their own right, but aren't by any means supercomputers. They're
each approximately the equivalent of a solid desktop build from 10 years ago or so (hence
the title of this hill). What I find is that many engineers (myself occasionally included,
I'll cop to it) tend to just throw code at the hardware expecting that it'll be fine.
Premature optimization, root of all evil, yadda yadda yadda. This often manifests in
writing electron apps (*insert vomit sound effect*).

Now, the electron app thing is actually not true of the system I work on. But, the
tendency to say "we use a fast compiled language, and my tests run pretty fast on my $4000
macbook, so it should be fine" is unnervingly high. I've spent a lot of time over the last
year working on how I architect software from a higher level rather than just ooga-ooga-monkey-type-code
all the time. This mostly resolves into [being data driven where possible](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WDIkqP4JbkE) and
[understanding the actual tech that underlies how we deal with data](https://www.oreilly.com/library/view/designing-data-intensive-applications/9781491903063/), but
I think there's a lot to be said too about just treating your dev machine as your
deployment machine (in cases like mine where they're markedly different in terms of
compute power).

I want my stuff to compile in seconds, not minutes (ideally in less than a second). Same
for running most test suites. Eventually, all code runs on real hardware. If you start out with
the mindset of being as optimal as possible with that hardware, you get better software.
So treat the hardware that you do have as worse than it is, and you'll produce systems
that run so fast they'll rip the skin from your bones (no, I will not insert a rust
"blazing fast" meme here).

Also, as a shoehorned in final point, one stick of RAM now costs the GDP of a small island
nation, so it's probably good to squeeze everything you can out of the chips you do have.
Fuck you, AI industry.

## [The best way to become a better SWE is to become a better technical writer]($section.id('opinion2'))
This one gets a little bit thorny because solid technical writing in many places often requires strong English.
Obviously, there are probably a couple people on the planet who don't speak that
language, or don't speak it as their mother tongue. I'm thinking there's at least three or
four people like that, probably.

Joking aside, this one comes from a true place of anecdotal experience (and translation
apps are usually good enough here too). The engineers who I have most respected, admired,
and learned from in my career have without exception been exceedingly competent writers,
and have not shied away from writing huge essays in team chats, design docs, readmes, or
really anywhere else with a text renderer. This is not to say that you need to write a
multi-thousand word essay for every little thing, but by writing something other
than a thumbs up emoji, you are thinking about the problem. This is a [well-known thing](https://www.nature.com/articles/s44222-025-00323-4),
of course. I'm not treading new ground here.

If I had to end with a "main thrust" though, so to speak, it's that I think the biggest
thing that has had an impact on my career and my skill as an engineer is building up a
willingness to write technically, even if it's often a chore (I have the whole system in
my head, dammit!). Not only for my peers, but for my future self as well. I will often
find myself coming back to a long message to a colleague, or an `Architecture.md` file I
wrote months or years ago, purely from some key word or phrase that I know exists in it.
And then I get a window into what I was thinking, why I was thinking it, and more often
than not a rebuttal to an idea I think that I've just had, but that I actually had all
that time ago and discarded. Maybe I should have, maybe not, but having the thought
written down is always valuable. This is a major piece of my career (and my own hobby
stuff) that I continue to try and focus on improving, no matter what.
